---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.svg";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
import { STAGES } from "../stages";
import "../styles/scientific.css";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="An abstract diagram combining a parallel-plate rheometer's radial graduations with a plotted flow curve, in the SlopU gold and ink palette"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2>The claim</h2>
  <p>
    Every question this course asks starts from the same premise: a food's
    texture is a measurable material property, governed by the same
    thermodynamics, transport equations and rheology used to characterise any
    soft material — not a matter of taste, and not beyond the reach of a
    model. A boba pearl's "QQ" bounce is a storage-to-loss modulus ratio. A
    spring roll's crunch is a material below its glass transition. Twelve
    modules build the vocabulary and the tools to say exactly which
    property, and why.
  </p>

  <h2>Four stages</h2>
  <p>
    The teaching period runs in four stages, each three modules long, each
    building directly on the last:
  </p>
  <ol class="stage-list">
    {
      STAGES.map((stage) => (
        <li>
          <strong>
            Stage {stage.number}: {stage.title}
          </strong>{" "}
          — {stage.description}
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ol>

  <h2>Two instruments, not two dozen</h2>
  <p>
    Rather than one simulation per module, the course concentrates its
    interactive work into two instruments used repeatedly across the
    Laboratory Portfolio: a <strong>Virtual Rheometer</strong> for flow
    behaviour, and a <strong>Crispness Phase Diagram</strong> for the
    glass-transition physics behind texture loss. Both are built to the same
    honesty standard as the written modules — labelled assumptions, visible
    fit quality, no invented data.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title="Course overview" href="/overview/">
      <p>Thesis, learning outcomes, prerequisites, and how the four stages fit together.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>All twelve teaching modules, grouped by stage.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>What is marked, what it is worth, and when it is due.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches the course and how to reach them.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .stage-list {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--at-spacing-sm);
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
  }
</style>
